WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.070 align:start position:0%
 
this is tomorrow reading from reviewer  stephen petit the play date is a tiny  little handheld filled with wonder and  games you can't find anywhere else with  24 season 1 games delivered in twos  across 12 weeks the playdate has a bunch  of great and inventive experiences out  of the gate understated one bit black  and white visuals and quirky crank  turning gameplay give the playdate a  unique charm but this isn't just a  novelty device the play date feels like  it has lasting power thanks to its  stellar design and open and inviting  approach to game development that will  undoubtedly lead to many more cool and  interesting games after the first season  wraps as you can probably tell already  the play date is very small it measures  in at approximately three by three  inches and is only a third of an inch  thick it has a 2.7 inch display which is  about the same size as a game boy  advanced screen while it doesn't  technically have a backlit screen it's  very reflective which gives it far more  vibrant image than older handhelds  without backlights that said you do need  to play in a fairly well lit area and  tilt the device towards the direction of  the light otherwise objects on screen  are shadowy like an out of focus camera  played as graphical and screen tech  limitations don't stop it from producing  some beautiful and expressive games that  make stellar use of black and white  colors the minimalist design extends to  the controls the play date has a d-pad  two face buttons and of course a crank  the sturdy crank has a metal magnetic  arm and a little pocket on the side of  the device that the handle slots into  allowing it to be stealthily stored away  when not in use you simply lift it up  into place and you're good to go it's a  seamless transition from play mode to  storage mode and vice versa the crank  has a full 360 degree range of motion  and it's utilized in different ways for  each game sometimes it's the main  control method while other times it's  just tied to one small mechanic you  could think of it like the play dates  version of an analog stick due to the  player small size and sometimes rapid  crank turning gameplay the most  comfortable way to hold it for long  sessions is with a claw grip it's easy  to see that it's not an ergonomically  designed handheld but by wrapping your  hands around the small frame it's  actually pretty cozy left-handed users  may have some issues though an  accessibility option lets you invert the  display turning everything backwards but  as you can tell the new location of the  buttons make this control method less  than ideal where the playdate excels is  with the pickup and play mentality it's  seemingly designed to be an always on  handheld with the lock screen showing a  clock when locked it's essentially in  sleep mode and drains very little  battery panic says the play date can  remain on the lock screen for 14 days  between charges whereas actual play time  is roughly 8 hours with a full charge a  prompt on the lock screen informs you  when new games are added as does the  flashing blue light on the lock button  new games are covered in wrapping paper  that makes a thrilling tada sound when  revealed  considering that it has a dock it would  make sense to have a built-in kickstand  unfortunately you'll need to purchase  the play date stereo dock or a small  display stand to prop it up like a clock  playdate has a simple and clean ui all  games are housed as cute little tiles on  the home menu the menu button pauses  your game and pulls up the sidebar that  can be used to instantly return to the  home screen adjust the volume or take a  screenshot the device settings menu  includes network details accessibility  options storage general stuff like that  while play date only has four gigabytes  of storage the games so far take up very  little space even with all 24 season 1  games installed my play date still had  3.7 gigabytes left since we didn't have  12 weeks to experience playdate season 1  we had an accelerated version of  playdate's cool approach to adding new  content two games were digitally  delivered to the play day each day for  12 days this made the play date part of  our daily routine and it was exciting to  have new games to try especially  considering that there are wildly  different experiences to be found on  playdate across a wide variety of genres  ratchetier is a superb top-down  action-adventure reminiscent of classic  zelda games you play as a mechanics  apprentice who has to save the day when  the town is overrun by monsters  [Music]  ratchet has traditional zelda  progression including hidden heart  pieces to find and the tried and true  dungeon setup with gadgets to find to  make use of the crank controls for  example sword spin attacks and  brightening the glow over lantern upper  form by twirling the crank omaze is a  clever puzzle game about maneuvering  through a series of circles it uses 360  degree controls with the crank while  also making use of buttons for  manipulating objects omaze is a great  example of how the crank can be used as  a primary control method to create  effect  [Music]  another game dub 360 is a smart  reimagining of breakout that allows you  to move the paddle on a circular plane  it turns breakout into thrilling chaos  and is filled with unique layouts that  give the classic a new layout strategy  [Music]  but not every game fares as well when  heavily relying on the crank a few games  such as the space game star sled and  hyper meteor require precise and quick  movements that can be frustrating with  the crank and make it feel gimmicky  while the crank is the feature that  makes the play date stand out as a piece  of hardware there are some standout  games that use the crank in very small  ways or not at all for instance demon  quest 85 is an excellent text adventure  revolving around piecing together clues  to summon demons inventory hero is a  resource management game that is fresh  and new thanks to its fast paced loop  that gives you the feeling of an action  game  and questy chess is a wonderful spin on  chess that has grid based levels to work  your way through it retains the  principles and rules of the classic  strategy game while turning it into a  clever tactics game with some seriously  great levels and puzzles to solve  some games are high score chasers that  are fun for quick spurts while others  have a conventional beginning and end  while we haven't completed all of the  games we found that a decent number of  them can be finished in a few hours but  even with more than 50 hours of time  spent with season 1 we've still found  ourselves going back for more and keep  in mind that season 1 is only the  beginning for play date panic designed  the play date to be developer friendly  and community focused anyone can create  a game for playdate and then upload it  to their handheld as well as share or  sell via storefront such as itch panic  has made the entire software development  kit available for free and there's also  a beginner-friendly browser game making  tool called pulp you can make small  games without any prior coding knowledge  and pop's language called pubscript is a  stripped-down coding language with heaps  of easy-to-follow documentation  available already the open nature of  playdate development fuels its future  potential pope isn't the only panic made  software for playdate there's also  mirror which is streaming software for  mac pc and linux the free program allows  you to connect your play date to your  computer to stream your games to sites  such as twitch it also doubles as a way  to play games on larger screens as the  on-screen simulator has a zoom function  that increases the field of view with  mirror you can still use the handheld  for controls or you can use a keyboard  or traditional controllers such as  playstation xbox controllers the analog  stick on your controller becomes the  crank the app has some bugs at the  moment including not recognizing  controller schemes sometimes as well as  larger issues such as screen stuttering  frame skips and input lag while the play  date excels as a compact portable it's  cool that the mirroring option is there  and hopefully improves over time as a  piece of hardware the playdate certainly  punches above its weight it has a  minimalistic design aesthetic that both  charms and gels with what playdate is  all about falling in love with games all  over again by diving into experiences  that feel both brand new and nostalgic  the seasons of games model is a fun way  to keep you coming back for new  surprises and the communal and open  approach to expanding the play date  library gives it enormous potential for  the future  [Music]

